### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The evidence from multiple sources confirms that Russia did send medical aid to the United States in the form of medical equipment to help coronavirus patients. A Russian military transport plane delivered 60 tons of supplies—including ventilators, masks, and respirators—to JFK airport in New York, accepted by the Trump administration following a phone call between Trump and Putin ([NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/russian-plane-coronavirus-medical-gear-lands-u-s-after-trump-n1174436)). Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov publicly confirmed the aid ([Anadolu Agency](https://www.aa.com.tr/en/health/russia-sends-medical-aid-to-us-to-help-fight-covid-19/1788290)). However, the shipment actually occurred in late March/early April 2020, not October 2020 as the claim's date suggests. The Russian-made ventilators were later returned after identical models caused fatal fires in Russian hospitals ([NPR](https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/05/21/860143691/u-s-sends-ventilators-to-russia-in-5-6-million-coronavirus-aid-package)), and Russia billed the US approximately $660,000 for the shipment ([ABC News](https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/russia-bills-us-660k-aid-included-gas-masks/story?id=70451912)). Despite the date discrepancy, the core factual content of the claim is well-supported by evidence.